Title: "What Is Checked"
Id: "8b5700cc-4d7d-480e-a424-eece3673778e"

Text: "`mlg check` runs a fixed pipeline and reports every problem it finds as an
error (the checker emits no warnings). It finishes with either
`Checked N file(s)` or `Found N issue(s).`. Before checking, unless `mlg.json`
sets `formatOnCheck` to `false`, the collection is formatted, and any top-level
item missing an `Id:` has one generated. The stages, in order, validate:

**Configuration (`mlg.json`).** The file must be readable, valid JSON, and a
JSON object; every field is required (`name`, `version`, `margin`,
`formatOnCheck`, `outputDir`); `name`/`version` are strings, `margin` is a
positive integer, `formatOnCheck` is a boolean, and `outputDir` is a non-empty
relative path inside the collection. The old key `print_margin` is rejected in
favor of `margin`.

**Collection and `toc` structure.** An explicit target must be a `.mlg` file
that is part of the collection. Every directory's `toc` is checked against its
real children: no duplicate entries, every listed name resolves, and every real
child is listed. `toc` lines must be well formed.

**Parsing.** Three layers each report their own errors: the line/indentation
proto layer (including rejecting single-quoted formulations), the structural
group/section layer (missing, out-of-order, or unexpected sections; unknown
top-level groups; heading, clause, formulation, and text-body shape), and the
formulation expression layer (token errors and construct-specific errors such
as `expected top-level ' is '`). `Refines:` documentation rejects `called:`,
`Topic:` documentation accepts only `called:`, and `represents:` entries must be
`\\\\coercion` or `\\\\encoding`.

**Prose code fences.** A ```` ```mlg ```` block inside a `Text:` value or
documentation is parsed as real source and reports a syntax error if it fails;
```` ```mlg-fragment ```` and non-`mlg` fences are skipped.

**Identity and uniqueness.** Every top-level item needs an `Id:` holding a
quoted UUID; Ids must be unique. At most one top-level `Writing:` item is
allowed. Command signatures must be unique across definition kinds — a
mapping-parameter header uses its specialized signature for this check, so
distinct specialized signatures may share one general signature. Each
operator/function key may have at most one `Disambiguates:`. Spec-infix headings
(`\\:...:/`) are allowed only on `Defines:`, refined headings (`::`) only on
`Refines:`.

**Documentation.** `Defines`, `Declares`, and `States` must include a `called:`
or `written:` item in `Documented:`; `Refines` must include an `adjective:`
item instead. Theorem-like groups require no such item.

**Command references.** Every command reference must resolve to a defined
signature with a matching argument shape (refined references may fall back to
their base command and refinement pieces), and command `when:`/context
requirements are checked at each use site — including the reduction target of an
`Enables:` `capability:`. Mapping-parameter references resolve among the
specialized definitions for their general signature and report an ambiguity when
no unique most-specific candidate exists.

**Target symbols.** Every parameter and target symbol a definition introduces
must be typed: a header parameter needs a `when:` fact; a `Defines` target
symbol must be specified directly or through `extends:`/`declares:`. A `Refines`
target symbol may also be inherited, so a symbol the refined base type already
declares counts as specified. A `Declares` target symbol must be assigned
exactly once and must state its type, either `... is <type>` or a top-level
build `\\<type>@<value>` — a bare `X := {...}` is rejected. Destructured
components (`M ::= (X, *)`) are typed from `extends:`/`via`, `declares:`, the
parameter's `when:` type, or the right-hand type of a `::=` binding, and need no
separate `when:` entry.

**`when:` clauses.** `when:` may only constrain the definition's own parameters,
and only with `<subject> is <type>` or `<subject> \"op\" <target>`.

**Specifications vs. statements.** An `is` specification or infix specification
introduces a symbol, so it is allowed only in binding positions (`exists:`,
`given:`, `forAll:`, `let:`, `where:`, `when:`, `suchThat:`); in a statement
position it must use the predicate form `is?` or `\\:...?:/`.

**Symbol scope.** Every referenced symbol must be introduced before use.
`::=` introduces symbols (including destructured components); `:=` does not — its
right-hand side must already be in scope.

**Spec facts and operators.** A spec fact `x \"op\" T` is valid only when `T`'s
type provides that operator, and infix spec signatures must be defined by
`Defines:`. Operators resolve in the order described under Type Resolution;
otherwise the operator is reported unresolved. Member access `M.*` must resolve
to a member of the owner.

**Capabilities, requirements, and builds.** `Requires:`/`Enables:` capabilities
are validated, including `from:` build-backed capabilities and `from: ... as:`
bindings; a provided spec operator's target must be the described item; a
`Requires:` `definition:` must reference a `Declares:` entry and establish its
stated fact. Build expressions `\\<type>@<value>` (coercion) and
`\\<type>@!<value>` (coercion + encoding) are checked, and `Enables:` `relation:`
declarations select that behavior through `represents: \\\\coercion` or
`represents: \\\\encoding`. Type predicates, function-type specifications, and the
arguments of `is` type statements are also checked."
Id: "0f8136db-1ce1-426d-b690-9ca405705e49"

SectionTitle: "Group-Specific Validations"
Id: "b47b0b1a-d99f-4bb5-9207-599e1d7ed483"

Text: "- **`Equivalent:`** — every `to:` item must be a command that uses the header's
  parameters directly, not expressions; all items must be the same definition
  kind; and their target shape, defined type, `when:` requirements, `extends:`
  type, and capabilities must agree across members.
- **`Refines:` `extends:`** — the `extends:` subject must match the `Refines:`
  subject, a `[[...]]` in it must name that subject, and a `Refines:` must have
  the form `Refines: <form>`.
- **`Refines:` refinement markers** — `implicitly:`/`explicitly:` take no
  arguments and are mutually exclusive; either requires the refined base type to
  be a subtype of another type; `implicitly:` additionally requires the body to
  contain only the inherited `extends:` clause and that clause to literally name
  the parent type's refinement, while `explicitly:` requires at least one
  property beyond it.
- **`Justification:`** — each entry's `have:` must restate the formulation
  carrying its `[:label:]`, and every entry must be referenced by some labeled
  formulation.
- **Function literals** — a parameter must be a name with a spec (`(x_ is ...)`)
  or a bare name whose type is already known from an `is`.
- **Function types** — function-type spec parameters must be written `_`, and a
  call must match the function's arity.
- **`satisfies`** — its right-hand side must be a specification.
- **Spec-operator targets** — the target of a `:->` spec operator must be a
  value, not a type.
- **Inferred parameters** — an `X?` argument may introduce `X` only once.
- **Variadic arguments** — a 2D parameter rejects ragged matrices, empty
  dimensions, flat comma-separated arguments, and 2D syntax in a non-curly
  argument group."
Id: "9b7883ee-4c65-4c28-995a-c8d6fe0af139"
